To enable logging for all outgoing HTTP requests in a Spring Boot application, you can use Spring's `WebClient` (reactive) or `RestTemplate` (blocking), both of which support custom `ClientHttpRequestInterceptor`s. These interceptors can log request and response details, which is useful for debugging and monitoring network activity.

Here's a practical approach using `WebClient`, which is the modern, recommended way to make HTTP calls in Spring Boot 2.5+ and simplifies code compared to `RestTemplate`:

### 1. Add Dependencies

If you haven't already, add WebFlux and Reactor Netty to your `build.gradle`:

```gradle
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-webflux'
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-logging'
```

### 2. Create a Request Logger Interceptor

```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.reactivestreams.Publisher;
import org.springframework.cloud.openfeign.RequestInterceptor;
import org.springframework.cloud.openfeign.ResponseInterceptor;
import org.springframework.http.client.ClientHttpRequestInterceptor;
import org.springframework.http.client.ClientHttpResponse;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientResponse;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientRequest;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient.ResponseSpec;

public class HttpLoggingInterceptor implements ClientHttpRequestInterceptor {

    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(HttpLoggingInterceptor.class);

    @Override
    public ClientResponse intercept(ClientRequest request, ClientResponseSpec next) {
        logger.info("=== HTTP REQUEST ===");
        logger.info("URL: {}", request.url());
        logger.info("Method: {}", request.method());
        request.headers().forEach((k, v) -> logger.info("Header: {} = {}", k, v));
        if (request.body() != null) {
            logger.info("Body: {}", request.body());
        }

        ClientResponse response = next.apply(request);

        logger.info("=== HTTP RESPONSE ===");
        logger.info("Status: {} {}", response.statusCode(), response.statusText());
        response.headers().forEach((k, v) -> logger.info("Response Header: {} = {}", k, v));
        String body = response.body() != null ? response.body().block() : "No body";
        logger.info("Body: {}", body);

        return response;
    }
}
```

### 3. Register the Interceptor with `WebClient`

```java
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientRequest;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientResponse;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.ClientHttpRequestInterceptor;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;

@Configuration
public class WebClientConfig {

    @Bean
    public WebClient webClient() {
        return WebClient.builder()
                .defaultHeader("Content-Type", "application/json")
                .interceptors(http -> http.addInterceptorLast(new HttpLoggingInterceptor()))
                .build();
    }
}
```

### 4. Use `webClient` in Your Services

```java
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import reactor.core.publisher.Mono;

@Service
public class MyService {

    private final WebClient webClient;

    public MyService(WebClient webClient) {
        this.webClient = webClient;
    }

    public Mono<String> callApi(String url) {
        return webClient.get().uri(url).retrieve().bodyToMono(String.class);
    }
}
```

